From cddf6c54ccd39b9f39e72d17b3e4ce1ff13aea33 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Pierre-Yves Le Borgne <pylaterreur@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 15:09:03 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] logd: don't hand out stale entries after the ring wraps

logd keeps the messages in a ring buffer. When a message does not fit
before the end of the buffer, log_add() writes an end marker (an entry
of size 0) where the current round ends and starts over at the
beginning, and "oldest" then points into the entries of the previous
round. As the new round grows, log_add() pushes "oldest" forward
through those entries, but nothing stops it at the end marker: the
entry after a zero-sized one is its header's own end, which is stale
data of an earlier round. From then on log_list() starts from garbage,
so "logread" gets entries with random sizes and timestamps, and logd
can read past the end of the buffer. musl's asctime_r() aborts on such
a timestamp, which shows up as

  traps: logread[...] general protection fault ... in libc.so

Messages of varying length make it likely, e.g. "logger" with 1 to 100
characters in a loop next to "logread -l 1" in a loop (FS#4118).

Stop at the end marker: when "oldest" reaches it, the oldest entry is
the first one of the new round, at the start of the buffer.

Also start over at the beginning when the header of the next entry does
not fit before the end of the buffer, not only when its first byte does
not: an entry that starts in the last bytes of the buffer has its header
cut, and log_list() reads past the end of the buffer there.

Assisted-by: Claude Code (Claude Opus 5.5)
Signed-off-by: Pierre-Yves Le Borgne <pylaterreur@gmail.com>
---
 log/syslog.c | 13 +++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/log/syslog.c b/log/syslog.c
index 7854523..17b7a6d 100644
--- a/log/syslog.c
+++ b/log/syslog.c
@@ -89,7 +89,8 @@ log_next(struct log_head *h, int size)
 {
 	struct log_head *n = (struct log_head *) &h->data[PAD(size)];
 
-	return (n >= log_end) ? (log) : (n);
+	/* an entry starts at log when its header doesn't fit before log_end */
+	return (n + 1 > log_end) ? (log) : (n);
 }
 
 static uint64_t
@@ -194,8 +195,16 @@ log_add(char *buf, int size, int source)
 	/* find new oldest entry */
 	next = log_next(newest, size);
 	if (next > newest) {
-		while ((oldest > newest) && (oldest <= next) && (oldest != log))
+		while ((oldest > newest) && (oldest <= next) && (oldest != log)) {
 			oldest = log_next(oldest, oldest->size);
+
+			/*
+			 * past the last entry of the previous round, what is left
+			 * is stale data: the oldest entry is at the start
+			 */
+			if ((oldest > newest) && !oldest->size)
+				oldest = log;
+		}
 	} else {
 		//fprintf(stderr, "Log wrap\n");
 		newest->size = 0;
-- 
